WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.810
- Hi, I'm Clio Doyle.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.850
I'm a sixth-year PhD candidate

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.890
in English and Renaissance Studies,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.540
and I'm a 2020 winner of the Field Prize.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.953
My prize submission is called:

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:22.710
“’Slimy Kempes Ill Smelling Of The Mud’:

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.110
The Terroir of Poetry

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.420
and the Desire for Change
in Barclay's Eclogues.”

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.990
It's about the 16th century
poet, Alexander Barclay.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.370
And, the way which his
work reflects this vision

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.010
of poetry as being created out of the food

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.280
that the poet eats,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.280
in this case, Barclay's speakers
are eating substandard food

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.810
and therefore, their
poetry is marred by this.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:54.480
Instead of seeing this as
necessarily a bad thing,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.023
I suggest in my essay that,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.450
poetry is necessarily shaped
by the material constraints

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.900
of its creation,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.460
but it can also in the very moment

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.310
in which it is distorted
by an imperfect world,

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.273
attest to the need for a better one.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.310
I was thinking when I read it a year ago

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.020
by the academic job market,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.690
but I think it also applies

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.670
to this even more imperfect world

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.900
that we're living in right now.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.243
Anyway, thank you.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.420
- Hello, my name is Daniel Eastman.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.700
I am graduating with a PhD

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.870
in the Department of Religious Studies.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.700
My work is on the social history

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.290
of early Christian communities, primarily

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.770
in the Eastern Mediterranean area.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.663
And I am a 2020 winner of the Field Prize.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.427
My prize submission is titled:

00:01:59.427 --> 00:02:01.500
"How To Read The Gospel(s):

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.303
Reading And Ritual In Late Ancient Syria.”

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.970
If you asked most historians
of early Christianity

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.140
who the most important actors were

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.500
in ancient Christian Society,

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.100
you might get a range of answers.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.760
Bishops or priests or
perhaps apostles, prophets

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.703
or other charismatic figures.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.650
One answer that you probably
would not get is books.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.690
Books, so the assumption
goes are not people.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.550
They don't go around and doing things,

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.200
acting on or against others,
and impacting the webs

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.803
of relationships that constitutes society.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.630
My article challenges this assumption

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:46.100
by focusing on a particular kind of book,

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.050
specifically, gospel
books written in Syriac

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.493
between the fifth and seventh centuries.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.180
I argue that these books were
actually major social actors

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.163
in early Christian communities
in Syria and Mesopotamia.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.330
To make this argument,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.240
I draw on a range of sources
not often examined together,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.140
including Syriac gospel book manuscripts,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.810
the surgical commentaries and
in geographical literature

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.610
and archeological evidence
concerning the structure

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.663
of church buildings in
the Ancient Near East.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.690
What I find in the article

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.710
is that gospel books played
a number of important roles

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.000
in Syriac Christian communities,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.380
including judging between
competing parties,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.330
ordaining new clergy, and even healing,

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.093
or sometimes killing
people whom they touched.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.970
In all of these roles Syriac Christians

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.240
understood gospel books to be acting as

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.680
the physical presence of Christ,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.580
whose life is recounted in the
four canonical gospels texts

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.493
contained within the gospel book.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.230
By tracing the connections
between the reading

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.690
of the gospel text and the appearance

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.430
and movement of the gospel
book within the temporal

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.880
and spatial context of the weekly liturgy.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.300
I argue that the status of the gospel book

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.300
as the person of Christ
was not simply a given,

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.210
it was rather a social
fact based on the web

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:19.840
of relationships construct
constructed around

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.310
the gospel book,

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:24.163
both inside and outside
the church building.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.710
For example, gospel books occupied

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.730
a special thrown like
structure in the middle

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:34.010
of many Syrian churches and
they precessed back and forth

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.770
within the church space,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:40.020
in a way meant to mirror the
historical movement of Christ

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.200
to and from Jerusalem.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.980
And outside the church building
gospel books often presided

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.940
over councils, again, seated on a throne

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.590
in the middle of the proceedings.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.330
In both settings,

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:56.550
the placement of the gospel
book vis-a-vis other structures

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.420
and people encouraged its viewers

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:04.990
to see it as the physical
manifestation of Christ's presence

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.513
and to relate to it accordingly.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:13.080
Although my article
focuses on gospel books,

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.590
these weren't the only
books or the only objects

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:19.150
that did things in
early Christian Society.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.360
Scholars today are just
starting to explore

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.060
the extent to which the
fabric of late Ancient Society

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.740
in general went beyond our
modern categories of human

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.283
and non-human subject and object.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.300
My hope is that the questions I raised

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.310
in this article will
stimulate further exploration

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.650
into the agency of objects writ large,

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.870
as well as shedding light
on the particular agency

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:46.443
of gospel books.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:52.220
My thanks to the members of
the Porter Field Committee

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.887
for reading the article
and to you for listening.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:11.070
- Hello, my name is Kevin Feeney.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.060
I'm graduating with a
Ph.D. in Ancient History

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.880
from the History Department here at Yale.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.250
And I'm a 2020 winner of the Porter Prize.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.310
My prize-winning submission is called

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:26.190
“Roman Imperial Accession
From Maximinus Thrax

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:29.440
To Justinian (235 to 527 CE)”

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.480
My work examines the age-old question

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.880
of where political legitimacy comes from,

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.380
and it takes an
interdisciplinary perspective

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:41.763
to apply this question
to the late Roman Empire.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:45.570
I find that rather than
operating on the basis

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:49.630
of an accepted universal
theory of succession.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.960
I mean instead, new Roman
emperors arose to meet

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.170
the challenges of particular moments,

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:58.210
whether that was out in the
field or at home in the capital.

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:01.150
With no accepted means
of arbitrating between

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:03.550
the competing claims
of different emperors.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:07.290
The only way to really establish
oneself as a secure emperor

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.783
was victory in warfare over rivals.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.730
Thank you, very much and best wishes,

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:16.070
to all my fellow 2020
graduates who are finishing up

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.920
under such difficult circumstances
that are in the world.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.140
- Hi everyone, I'm Ayten Tartici.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.240
I graduated in December 2019

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.250
with a PhD from the Comparative
Literature Department.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:39.180
I'm a literary critic and
a scholar of modernism

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.480
and a 2020 winner of the
John Addison Porter Prize.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:43.740
My price submission,

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:48.250
which was also my dissertation
is called “Adagios Of Form.”

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.150
In my work, I study how slowness

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.650
is both a surprisingly, persistent theme

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.100
and a striking source of
aesthetic innovation in art,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.510
literature, music, and film from the 20th

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.180
and 21st century.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.780
It turns out that slowness
is intimately tied

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:05.400
to questions of community and class.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.870
We often disapprovingly
think we're slow as a result

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:12.430
of living together with
others in comparative context,

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.720
yet writers as diverse
as F.Scott Fitzgerald,

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.930
the French novelist, Marcel Proust,

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:20.490
and the Italian modernist Italo Svevo,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.880
teach us that taking one's time

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.270
can instead signal expertise,
political resistance,

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:29.320
class consciousness, and even creativity.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:30.640
Those same aesthetic

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.510
and thematic concerns
remain highly relevant today

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:34.940
to cultural developments

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:38.543
and experimental music,
art, food, and cinema.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:43.030
To the graduating Class
of 2020 congratulations

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:45.250
on all your accomplishments.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.590
I'm honored to share in these celebrations

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:48.910
and cannot wait to see

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.133
what the future holds for each of you.

